`_infer_concat_order_from_coords` starts with `tile_ids = [() for ds in datasets]`, so `len(tile_ids)` initially equals the number of input datasets. Inside `for dim in ds0.dims:`, `tile_ids` is either unchanged or rebuilt by the list comprehension `[...] for tile_id, position in zip(tile_ids, order, strict=True)`, which produces one new entry per old `tile_id`; because `order` is also derived from one value per dataset, this preserves the list length. So the invariant holds at every checked iteration. The two executed tests call the function once with 2 datasets and once with 4 datasets, giving distinct observed `len(tile_ids)` values of 2 and 4.

## Answer
{"holds_across_all_loop_iterations":true,"observed_tile_ids_lengths":[2,4]}
